  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 1245 AM MST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nClouds and cool temps will linger Sunday on the plains, as Srn\nPlains upper low is slow to move eastward, while mountains and\ninterior valleys see more sun and slightly warmer temps. Dry and\nwarmer all areas on Mon, as upper ridge moves across and low/mid\nlevel flow turns more swly. Next upper wave moves though late\nTue into Wed, though system continues to look rather\nprogressive/fast moving, which will likely limit precip amounts\nover srn CO. As wave approaches, lee surface trough on the \nplains will promote deep mixing and warmer temperatures both Tue\nand Wed, with readings in the 60s along and east of I-25, and\npotentially approaching 70f at a few locations across the lower \nArkansas Valley. Mountains, especially along the Continental \nDivide, will see a quick shot of snow Tue night/Wed morning, \nbefore precip ends by late day Wed as wave moves east. Suspect \nwe'll see accumulating snow and perhaps some brief travel issues\nover the higher mountain passes, though not looking at major \nimpacts at this point. \n\nDeeper upper trough then moves into the western U.S. late week,\nbefore ejecting eastward through the Rockies and into the plains\nlate Fri into Sat. System has potential to be impactful with\ndeep surface low spinning up over the High Plains and strong\nwinds/heavy snow developing, though many questions remain \nconcerning the ultimate track of the system and the location of \nthe heaviest precip. Model trends have been slowly shifting \nnorthward the past 24 hrs or so, with surface low spinning up \nslightly too far north to bring heavy snow to srn CO, though \nquick look at 14/00z ECMWF deterministic model shows it could be\nclose. Still rather far out of get an exact track at this \npoint, but system needs to be monitored as it won't take much of\na southerly shift to bring heavy snow/wind to srn CO.",
